  "note": "Re-judged 2026-10-10 against RFC 4303 S2.1 \"The SPI value of zero (0) is reserved for local, implementation-specific use and MUST NOT be sent on the wire.\" after e2e724cf57 changed TestResponderRefusesPeerSPIZero: it now asserts no first Child SA, an IKE_AUTH response with no SA payload and a NO_PROPOSAL_CHOSEN refusal, and no longer the IKE SA state, which RFC 7296 S2.21.2 governs and this row does not. The SPI is still isolated (same re-encrypted request as TestResponderUsesPeerSPI, which pins outbound 0x0a0b0c0d), and removing both refusals (selectAuthChildSA, createFirstChildSA) builds a Child SA on SPI 0 and reddens it. Every other producer unchanged and proven both ways: own SPI TestGenerateESPSPISkipsTheReservedZero (three zero draws, spi != 0 mutant recorded); peer SPI initiator SAr2, rekey request and rekey response refuse 0 with no Child SA and no ESP state and install exactly 0x0a0b0c0d; OSPF TestIPsecSPIZeroRefused refuses spi 0 and accepts 4294967295 (mutant recorded). Caveats, not gaps: TestGenerateESPSPI cannot fail on its own, and TestIPsecSPIBoundary's 255/256 pair tests the IANA 1..255 reservation, a neighbouring rule.",
